Shortest Path in an Undirected Graph — Which Algorithm to Use
It depends entirely on the type of edge weights:

Scenario	Algorithm	Time Complexity	Why
Unweighted (all edges = 1)	BFS	O(V + E)	Layer-by-layer expansion naturally finds shortest hops
Weighted (non-negative)	Dijkstra's	O((V + E) log V)	Greedy — always expands the cheapest unvisited node via a min-heap
Weighted (with negatives, no negative cycles)	Bellman-Ford	O(V × E)	Relaxes all edges V−1 times; handles negative weights safely
Quick Decision Flowchart
Is the graph unweighted (or all weights = 1)?
├── YES → Use BFS ✅  (fastest, simplest)
└── NO → Are all weights ≥ 0?
    ├── YES → Use Dijkstra's ✅
    └── NO → Are there negative-weight edges?
        └── YES → Use Bellman-Ford ✅




✅ Use BFS When:
Shortest path in unweighted graphs (its superpower)
Level-order traversal (e.g., tree levels, "minimum moves" problems)
Target is likely close to the source (BFS finds it faster without diving deep)
Multi-source BFS — e.g., "rotting oranges," "nearest gate" problems
✅ Use DFS When:
Cycle detection — tracking the recursion stack makes it natural (like your 2_dfs_adj_matrix_directed_graph_cycle.py)
Topological sort — DFS post-order gives you the ordering
Connected components / island counting — just flood-fill each component
Path existence — "does ANY path exist?" (not shortest, just yes/no)
Backtracking problems — permutations, combinations, maze solving with constraints
Graph is very deep but narrow — DFS uses less memory than BFS here